<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Resources the shrinker cannot see.

  A release build runs AGP's resource shrinker, which keeps a resource only if
  something references it statically — from code, from the manifest, or from
  another resource. `ic_stat_heart` is named by a *string* in Dart
  (`_androidIcon` in lib/core/env/notifications.dart), so nothing static points
  at it and it was dropped. The shrinker said so plainly in its own report,
  `build/app/outputs/mapping/devRelease/resources.txt`:

      drawable:ic_stat_heart:2131230850 is not reachable.

  What that cost: `initNotifications` threw at startup —

      PlatformException(invalid_icon, The resource ic_stat_heart could not be
      found. Please make sure it has been added as a drawable resource to your
      Android head project.)

  — unhandled, fatal, caught only by Sentry. So notifications were dead in every
  release build while working perfectly in debug, which is exactly the shape of
  bug that gets shipped: the icon is present in the debug APK, and the debug
  build is what anyone tests.

  Any future resource named only from Dart belongs in this list.

  `default_web_client_id` is the same bug with a different victim, and it cost
  more. The google-services Gradle plugin generates it from the web OAuth
  client in `google-services.json`, and `google_sign_in_android` reads it *by
  name* at runtime to use as the `serverClientId`. Nothing references it
  statically, so the shrinker dropped it from every release build and Google
  sign-in failed on the store app with

      GoogleSignInException(clientConfigurationError,
                            serverClientId must be provided on Android)

  while working perfectly in debug. 317 events across 21 users before anyone
  connected the two. Its siblings survive only because Play Services ships keep
  rules for them — the shrinker report lists `gcm_defaultSenderId`,
  `google_api_key` and `google_app_id` as "reachable from keep xml file" and
  `default_web_client_id` as "is not reachable". That asymmetry is the whole
  bug: three of the four generated strings are protected by someone else, and
  the fourth is ours to protect.
-->
<resources
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    tools:keep="@drawable/ic_stat_heart,@string/default_web_client_id" />
